{"parentUuid":"6453fbc4-5eef-4ef5-97c5-2002a3ddfcfb","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfzuc73kngKznDQM64tyQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The session got killed again, likely OOM or a restart, exit 137. The publish script already succeeded and outputs landed in site/public/media/tour/, while the CRF 25 comparison job died under memory pressure, which isn't critical since the CRF 22 pass is what matters.\n\nLooking at the actual encode: the web version comes out to 18.7 MB for 74 seconds, versus the existing walkthrough-en.mp4 at 9.5 MB for 1:53 — a bit large but reasonable at roughly 2 Mbps for 1080p video served on demand. I'm weighing whether CRF 24 would trim the size further, but 18.7 MB with lazy preload is acceptable for a product tour and I don't want to over-engineer this with more comparison passes that risk killing the session again. I'm also second-guessing whether `-tune stillimage` was the right encoding flag choice given this footage has actual motion rather than static content.\n\nNow I need to confirm the published tour files survived the restart intact, clean up any leftover ffmpeg processes or partial encode files in /tmp, and move on to wiring the tour into the site: a landing page TourSection plus hero link, the studio guide recording entry with transcript and chapters, developer docs covering the fal provider and tour video, and README/development doc updates. After that I'll run the full site and root test suites, clean up leftover footage and audio source files along with gitignore entries, and save progress notes.\n\nGiven the restarts, I want to work efficiently and detach any long-running tasks. For the TourSection design, I'm thinking elegant and designer-forward rather than generic cards -- a Bodoni heading, a short descriptive line, the video framed in a custom \"stage\" with its own poster and play button, seven chapter markers (Measure, Fitting room, Baste, Fit, Cut, Talk, Yours) that seek the video, captions on by default, and a transcript disclosure, all matching the site's paper-and-stitching aesthetic. I'm checking how existing sections structure their styles so I can follow the same convention for this new component.\n\nI'm noticing the codebase mixes patterns -- global.css classes for meet, inline Panda css() for hero and closing, and a scoped stylesheet with useStyles$ for material-room. Since the tour section needs a lot of rules including media queries, I'll go with the scoped stylesheet approach to keep things organized rather than bloating global.css. Now I'm working out the exact timing breakdown for the seven video chapters to build the chapter rail.\n\nI should use the visual chapter starts, which come slightly before the voice: 0, 8.6, 18.85, 26.85, 36.7, 44.95, 54.88, 63.5. Since \"Measure\" is chapter 01 but the \"Why\" intro precedes it, I can label the first chapter \"Why\" starting at 0.\n\nBut the video rail only shows seven chapters, Measure through Yours, so adding an eighth label would mismatch. Maybe the simplest approach is to keep the seven video chapters as-is, with Measure starting at 0:09 and the intro segment left unlabeled as just the start of the video.\n\nSo the chapter timestamps would be Measure 0:09, Fitting room 0:19, Baste 0:27, Fit 0:37, Cut 0:45, Talk 0:55, Yours 1:04 — with the video's opening \"why\" intro at 0:00 sitting before the first chapter, which is a common pattern for chapter lists.\n\nNow I'm thinking it's better to just start \"Measure\" at 0:00 so clicking it doesn't skip the hook, since the intro naturally leads into the measuring theme anyway. I'll skip the native `<track kind=\"chapters\">` approach since browsers don't render it well, and instead build custom chapter buttons that seek the video and highlight the active chapter on timeupdate.\n\nNow I'm turning to the landing tour section's background color, deciding between reusing the paper tone or differentiating it slightly from the meet section's shade.\n\nI recall there's a fabric-gl shader background that flickers and turns black on resize, so sections are likely transparent over it. I should check the hero bottom and meet top to see how they interact with it.\n\nSince Read doesn't compress files like Bash does, I'll use Read for docs/index.tsx to get the full content. Now I'm sketching the TourSection markup, starting with a section header labeled \"The tour · 1:14\" and a display heading introducing the fitting demo.\n\nFor the TourPlayer, I'm planning a figure with a video element (controls, lazy preload, poster image), a default captions track, a large custom play overlay before first playback, and a chapter list below that seeks and plays specific timestamps -- updating the active chapter on timeupdate, plus a collapsible transcript. I'm leaning toward native video controls paired with the custom chapter list for accessibility and practicality, with a big centered play button overlay.\n\nI'll hide native controls until the video starts, showing only the custom overlay button on the poster, then reveal controls once clicked and playback begins -- accepting that this interaction depends on JS, which is fine since Qwik's client always has it available.\n\nFor responsiveness, the video stays 16:9 at full width while the chapter list collapses into a horizontal scroll or two-column grid on mobile. Since the tour includes narration, I'll skip autoplay entirely to avoid the muted-autoplay requirement. I also want to add a \"Watch the tour\" link near the hero's existing \"Meet a persona ↓\" and \"Start your own fitting\" links, so I'm checking the hero's current link styles to match them.\n\nRather than replacing \"Meet a persona ↓\", I'll keep it since the persona conversation is a key feature, and instead add a small play-icon text link reading something like \"▶ Watch the 74-second tour.\" I'm also rethinking the studio guide's recording options: the new tour becomes the default entry, and I need to rename the existing ones (like \"Narrated tour · English\") to something clearer, such as \"October walkthrough · English,\" to avoid confusion with the new tour label.\n\nI need the note text itself to be bilingual, explaining English narration with French captions. For the transcript, I'm thinking of structuring it as a tour.json with chapter-based paragraphs, pairing each English narration line with its French translation.\n\nI'm deciding where this file should live — since both the docs tour page and the landing TourSection need it, a shared location like a lib module makes more sense than keeping it only in docs/, even though other doc components use JSON files there.\n\nI need to decide how chapter titles map to the 8 scenes versus 7 chapters, since Measure will need to absorb both the \"why\" and \"measure\" narration paragraphs. I'm sketching the structure for tour-recording.json with file path, poster, duration, and chapter entries containing id, start time, and English/French text.\n\nI'm also working out the French translations for chapter titles, like \"Mesurer\" for Measure and \"Cabine d'essayage\" for Fitting room, while weighing options for how to translate \"Baste\" in a sewing context.\n\nGood, the tailoring terms translate nicely: Fitting room → \"Cabine d'essayage\", Baste → \"Faufiler\", Fit → \"Ajuster\", Cut → \"Couper\". Now I'm checking that the French transcript paragraphs read naturally when cue lines are joined into full sentences, starting with the \"why\" and \"measure\" scenes.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":139843,"cache_read_input_tokens":92841,"output_tokens":12020,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":11709},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":139843,"ephe