{"parentUuid":"4a70bd72-a55b-457b-b9e2-0549b7008574","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4f5ed931-9f1d-475a-be32-8d6a81df3ec7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EGzhVctyjituRQMEx16Tv6","type":"tool_result","content":"1:@layer reset, base, tokens, recipes, utilities;\n3:/*\n17:}\n19:/* ─── Atelier ground ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n22:@layer base {\n95:}\n97:/* ─── Action verbs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n108:@layer recipes {\n251:}\n253:/* ─── Basting stitch — the signature mark ───────────────────────────────\n255:@layer base {\n409:}\n411:/* ─── Fitting — a scope dressed entirely in one persona's tokens ────────\n413:@layer base {\n501:}\n503:/* ─── Reduced motion: keep the craft, drop the movement ─────────────── */\n504:@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n511:}\n513:/* Unlayered so component utility classes cannot replace semantic cursors. */\n556:/* Companion conversation follows the recovered workroom studies: a visible\n643:@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {\n648:}\n649:@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n652:}\n671:@media (min-width: 1024px) {\n673:}\n674:@media (max-width: 767px) {\n702:}\n703:@media (pointer: coarse) {\n707:}\n708:@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n711:}\n\n/* ─── Basting stitch — the signature mark ───────────────────────────────\n   Long loose red stitches. Used for rules, outlines and \"sewn-in\" reveals. */\n@layer base {\n  .stitch-rule {\n    height: 2px;\n    border: 0;\n    background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--thread) 0 12px, transparent 12px 20px);\n    background-size: 20px 2px;\n    background-repeat: repeat-x;\n  }\n  .stitch-rule--chalk {\n    background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--chalk) 0 4px, transparent 4px 9px);\n    background-size: 9px 1px;\n    height: 1px;\n  }\n\n  .stitch-box {\n    position: relative;\n    :is(:disabled, [aria-disabled=\"true\"]),\n    label:has(input:disabled) { cursor: not-allowed; }\n    :is(:disabled, [aria-disabled=\"true\"]) * { cursor: inherit; }\n  }\n\n\n/* Companion conversation follows the recovered workroom studies: a visible\n   character, open exchanges and one generous writing surface. */\n.meet-section { background: #f8f6f0; scroll-margin-top: 76px; }\n.meet-layout { max-width: 1240px; margin-inline: auto; padding: 48px 32px 40px; }\n.meet-intro { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 48px; margin-bottom: 28px; }\n711 src/global.css","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"20677aa1-e97b-4341-be68-207e0553f3db","timestamp":"2026-10-13T21:02:08.135Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1:@layer reset, base, tokens, recipes, utilities;\n3:/*\n17:}\n19:/* ─── Atelier ground ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n22:@layer base {\n95:}\n97:/* ─── Action verbs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n108:@layer recipes {\n251:}\n253:/* ─── Basting stitch — the signature mark ───────────────────────────────\n255:@layer base {\n409:}\n411:/* ─── Fitting — a scope dressed entirely in one persona's tokens ────────\n413:@layer base {\n501:}\n503:/* ─── Reduced motion: keep the craft, drop the movement ─────────────── */\n504:@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n511:}\n513:/* Unlayered so component utility classes cannot replace semantic cursors. */\n556:/* Companion conversation follows the recovered workroom studies: a visible\n643:@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {\n648:}\n649:@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n652:}\n671:@media (min-width: 1024px) {\n673:}\n674:@media (max-width: 767px) {\n702:}\n703:@media (pointer: coarse) {\n707:}\n708:@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n711:}\n\n/* ─── Basting stitch — the signature mark ───────────────────────────────\n   Long loose red stitches. Used for rules, outlines and \"sewn-in\" reveals. */\n@layer base {\n  .stitch-rule {\n    height: 2px;\n    border: 0;\n    background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--thread) 0 12px, transparent 12px 20px);\n    background-size: 20px 2px;\n    background-repeat: repeat-x;\n  }\n  .stitch-rule--chalk {\n    background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--chalk) 0 4px, transparent 4px 9px);\n    background-size: 9px 1px;\n    height: 1px;\n  }\n\n  .stitch-box {\n    position: relative;\n    :is(:disabled, [aria-disabled=\"true\"]),\n    label:has(input:disabled) { cursor: not-allowed; }\n    :is(:disabled, [aria-disabled=\"true\"]) * { cursor: inherit; }\n  }\n\n\n/* Companion conversation follows the recovered workroom studies: a visible\n   character, open exchanges and one generous writing surface. */\n.meet-section { background: #f8f6f0; scroll-margin-top: 76px; }\n.meet-layout { max-width: 1240px; margin-inline: auto; padding: 48px 32px 40px; }\n.meet-intro { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 48px; margin-bottom: 28px; }\n711 src/global.css","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4a70bd72-a55b-457b-b9e2-0549b7008574","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/site","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"20677aa1-e97b-4341-be68-207e0553f3db","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_01EGzhVctyjituRQMEx16Tv6","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2735606a-ea7e-48a7-a835-f7b672f35988","timestamp":"2026-10-13T21:03:47.197Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/site","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"2735606a-ea7e-48a7-a835-f7b672f35988","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfzuqWxJZRuSFeh1bd23M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm thinking through the TourSection design, keeping consistent with the site's paper-and-stitch aesthetic—framing the tour video within a section that has a stitch-rule header, a small mono label like \"The tour · 1:14,\" and a Bodoni display headline teasing the fitting video.\n\nFor copy, I'm leaning into the tailoring metaphor with a headline like \"From measurements to a *finished* cut,\" paired with a short paragraph describing the fitting narrative across its chapters: measure, baste, fit, cut, talk, yours.\n\nBelow the video, I want clickable chapter chips with timecodes that seek the video to that point when clicked, plus highlighting the active chapter as playback progresses—both requiring lightweight client-side handlers in Qwik.\n\n`onTimeUpdate$` maps to the native timeupdate event, which fires frequently but is cheap since Qwik lazy-loads the handler once and caches it; using this declarative event binding instead of a `useVisibleTask$` listener keeps the whole thing lazy rather than eager.\n\nI should set `default` on the caption track matching the landing page's language so captions appear automatically for accessibility. For the landing video itself, autoplay doesn't make sense since it has narration and autoplay-with-sound is blocked anyway, so I'll use a poster image with controls and `preload=\"none\"` for efficiency since the file is faststart-encoded.\n\nI'm also considering a custom styled play overlay instead of relying solely on native controls — a button sitting on top of the poster that triggers playback and reveals the native controls on click, similar to the MotionStudy pattern, for a more polished feel. Setting up the play button state...\n\nBut I need to think through the no-JS fallback case, since Qwik requires JS to handle the click, so I should make sure the video still functions reasonably for users without JavaScript enabled.\n\nI'm leaning toward just always rendering native `controls` for robustness, keeping an overlay play button centered that gets hidden once playback starts, even though the native control bar on the poster might look a bit busy alongside it.\n\nSince this is a Qwik app where nearly all users have JS enabled, and the MotionStudy section already uses a custom overlay button without native controls, I'll match that existing pattern for consistency, then add a chapter list below that jumps playback to specific timestamps when clicked.\n\nI'm also weighing whether to add a hero link like \"Watch the tour (1:14)\" alongside the existing \"Meet a persona\" and \"Start your own fitting\" CTAs, but three calls to action might be too crowded — leaning toward a subtle text link rather than a full third button.\n\nI'll place it as a secondary text link with a small play icon, keeping \"Meet a persona ↓\" as primary and \"Start your own fitting\" tertiary, and add the TourSection right after the hero, before the \"Meet\" section.\n\nFor the Studio guide, I'm adding a 'tour' recording option as the default state alongside 'current', 'en', and 'fr', with labeled buttons for each recording's duration and language.\n\nNow I'm working out naming so the new tour doesn't clash with the existing \"Narrated tour · English\" entry from the older long walkthrough recorded October 2 — maybe renaming those older ones to something like \"Full walkthrough\" while the new 1:14 piece gets its own distinct label, something like \"Why Baste\" or similar that fits the picker's style.\n\nSettling on \"Why Baste · 1:14\" as the new button label, since that captures the user's own framing of what the video shows — why you'd use it and what it's like. I'll keep the aside text simple, pointing people to either the short tour, the current worktable, or the longer narrated walkthrough.\n\nNow I'm pulling together the tour data: I need an 8-scene structure with titles and narration in both English and French, built from the caption transcripts grouped by scene — things like the \"why\" scene about taste not fitting demographic buckets, and the \"measure\" scene about Baste starting from one person's films, music, and places.\n\nLet me double-check that each French cue lines up in meaning with its corresponding English cue at the same index, cross-referencing the English lines I captured so far.\n\nThe output continues with icons, ambient loops, and design tokens for CSS, Tailwind, or Panda, plus the ability to ask the persona why they feel the way they do about a design. I'm wrapping up the pitch: bring your own people, measure them in the Studio, and cut something that fits — but I notice my cue list split changed from an earlier version, so I need to double check consistency with what came before.\n\nChecking the word counts for the split groups confirms the 9-word threshold rule is working correctly, breaking at the \"and\" conjunction to keep each cue within limits. I'm verifying the total cue count matches 32 to align with my French translation, since the build process checks for equal line counts between languages.\n\nChecking cue 17: \"and a vision judge looks at every one.\" The group \"warm and cool, sparse and dense,\" — the pause at \"cool,\" only has group length 3, not enough to flush (needs ≥4), so I keep going to find the next valid break point.\n\nNow I'm checking the chapter clips mapping — generate, tokens, conversation, and new-persona each pointing to their respective mp4 files, and considering whether the tokens clip from the Oct 10 recording should replace the static tokens.png screenshot in the figure.\n\nFor the video treatment, I'm leaning toward muted, looping, playsInline video elements with preload=\"none\" and a poster image set to the still frame, using a manual play button overlay rather than autoplay so reduced-motion preferences are respected.\n\nI'll build this as a GuideClip component with a play/pause toggle overlay and a descriptive aria-label, since the clips are silent UI demos without need for captions. I also need to resolve whether the French figcaptions should flag that the screen recordings are in English, since the existing screenshots appear to be English-only despite the French locale.\n\nFor the fitting and orientation chapters, I'll keep the existing screenshots since the tour's fitting footage comes from the landing page rather than the Studio, making it unsuitable there. For downloads, I'm wiring up \"Download recording\" to the mp4 file and \"Download captions\" to the per-language vtt file, along with a recording note stating the tour is 74 seconds with English narration and bilingual captions, recorded October 10, 2026.\n\nNow I need to handle the video track and aspect ratio: the tour should include both English and French caption tracks with the default matching the guide's language, use the dedicated poster image, and since it's 16:9 rather than the other recordings' 16:10, I'm adding a data attribute so the CSS can apply a wide aspect ratio specifically for the tour video instead of letterboxing it.\n\nFor the transcript, I'm mapping out the scene titles in both languages for the tour's chapters—why, measure, fitting, baste, fit, cut, talk, and yours—making sure the French translations carry the right tailoring terminology, like \"bâtir\" for baste. Next I need to check the developer docs page to add sections for the fal provider and the tour video, then move on to updating the README.\n\nI'm also considering whether a shared tour data module makes sense—something like a TypeScript file exporting chapters with timecodes and bilingual text, so both the landing page's TourSection and the Studio guide's transcript stay synced. Ideally the publish script would generate this JSON manifest automatically so the timings never drift out of sync between the video and the written chapters.\n\nFor titles, I'm debating whether to keep them in the JSON alongside the text content or leave them as presentational labels in the component itself, since the chapter titles originate from the rail labels defined in build.mjs.\n\nI realize there are only 7 chapters for 8 scenes since \"why\" is the unlabeled intro, so I'm working out the chapter start times for Measure, Fitting room, Baste, Fit, Cut, Talk, and Yours. For publish.mjs to generate French text per scene, I need it to assign each caption cue to the scene whose time window contains the cue's start, which I can compute directly from the VTT files.\n\nI'm now drafting the tour.json structure with src, poster, duration, recorded date, and a scenes array holding per-scene English/French text, plus a bilingual title map keyed by scene id (why, measure, fitting, baste, fit, cut, talk) since build.mjs's CHAPTERS constant isn't exported for reuse.\n\nI'm deciding where this JSON file should live—likely `site/src/lib/tour.json` alongside the other lib JSON imports, since docs already rely on `resolveJsonModule`—and now thinking through whether the TourSection component should use global CSS like meet-section or a separate stylesheet approach.\n\nOpting for a scoped CSS file imported via `useStyles$`, I'm sketching the section markup: a kicker label, a headline about the \"seventy-four second\" fitting process, and a lead paragraph explaining the measure-baste-cut workflow.\n\nThe stage below holds the video player with a play button overlay and an ordered list of timestamped chapters as a seekable index. On desktop I want the video large on the left with chapters as a mono-font vertical rail on the right, wrapping below the video on mobile.\n\nI'm considering giving the video frame a dashed red \"basting\" outline to match the brand's stitch-box styling, and want to check the existing `.stitch-box` class to reuse it. The play button itself should be a dark pill reading \"▶ Play the tour 1:14.\"\n\nFor interactivity, I'm planning an SVG triangle instead of a glyph for rendering consistency, letting the global button cursor rules apply automatically. Chapter highlighting will track video time via `onTimeUpdate$`, matching the active chapter by finding the last one whose start time has passed, while seeking will set `currentTime` and resume playback -- though I need to double check how that interacts with `preload=\"none\"`.\n\nSince readyState HAVE_NOTHING still lets the browser queue the seek until metadata loads, that approach should work fine across browsers. I also want clicking a chapter before playback starts to flip the `started` flag so controls appear, and I'm considering accessibility touches like a descriptive aria-label and maybe a transcript detail for the narration.\n\nNow I need to check how the word-reveal effect in reveal.tsx handles nested elements like an `<em>` inside an h2, and figure out what `data-scroll` is doing on the meet section before touching the hero link.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1227,"cache_read_input_tokens":221073,"output_tokens":10344,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":10143},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1227,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10344,"cache_read_input_tokens":221073,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1227,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1227},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":96565,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"d4fa4eae-8319-4178-b020-5a1be60c8972","timestamp":"2026-10-1