{"parentUuid":"7562c53d-798e-4622-998c-a01c4eb51ee2","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfzvAAktwJodAnjXRXqD7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I lost the index.md5 checksum file when /tmp got cleared during the restart, so I can't directly verify index.html's content against what was last rendered. The only build.mjs changes since then were to vtt() and the French captions block, which shouldn't affect the HTML output, but I still want to confirm index.html's content didn't actually shift.\n\nTracing back: the render happened at 16:36–16:38 with index.html built around 16:27, and the subsequent build.mjs edits (vtt splitting, French captions, VTT positioning) all passed md5sum checks afterward, so the rendered HTML matches. The only remaining change only touches the vtt line, so I'm confident it's fine.\n\nNow I'm turning to the landing page TourSection design — since the user dislikes \"ugly cards,\" I want something editorial: a large video in a stitched frame, a chapter-marker list that seeks the video, and brief supporting text. I'm sketching out the video element with controls, a poster, captions track, and a custom play-button overlay since autoplay with sound isn't viable.\n\nI'm planning the chapter list as buttons that set currentTime and play the video, with the active chapter highlighted as playback progresses, keeping in mind the rule that components shouldn't set cursor styles directly. For page structure, I'm placing the new TourSection (#tour) right after the HeroSection, with a \"Watch the tour\" link added to the hero, and considering background tone (perhaps a deeper paper shade) to visually separate it from the hero and meet sections.\n\nThe video's \"Moss, measured\" tape-yellow motif gives me the idea to style the chapter scrubber itself as a tape measure—a horizontal strip with tick marks and labels positioned proportionally, clickable to seek. On mobile this gets cramped with 8 labels, so I'll fall back to a two-column grid instead. Starting to structure the tour section markup now.\n\nI want to add a transcript link to the Studio guide, and I'm double-checking the honesty of the claim that every screen is a real recording — the site footage and the Studio demo-mode replay of an actual run both qualify, and the personas are explicitly labeled as authored characters, so the framing holds up.\n\nNow I'm working out the tape measure layout: ticking every second across 74 ticks, with chapter markers positioned by percentage of the total duration. I'm checking whether chapter labels like \"Fitting room\" will fit at different breakpoints, planning to switch to uppercase mono labels on tablet and a grid layout below 768px if space gets too tight.\n\nA more robust approach is to use a flex row where each chapter's flex-grow matches its duration, so segment widths are proportional to time and labels never overlap — similar to YouTube's chapter bar, collapsing into a grid on narrow screens. Each segment acts as a button showing time and title with a progress fill for the active chapter, updating via a progress variable and marking the current step with aria-current, driven by time updates or a render loop.\n\nI'm thinking through smoothing the fill animation with a short linear CSS transition on the scale transform, disabled under reduced motion, and double-checking how Qwik maps event handler props like onTimeUpdate$ to actual DOM event names.\n\nSo Qwik lowercases the event name entirely, meaning `onTimeUpdate$` maps to \"timeupdate,\" matching the native DOM event name, consistent with how `onEnded$` maps to \"ended.\" I want to verify this against how the codebase actually uses `onTimeUpdate$` elsewhere.\n\nThinking through the chapter button behavior: clicking it sets `video.currentTime` and calls `play()`, which should work fine once metadata loads since preload=\"metadata\" only fetches a small chunk, and faststart ensures that happens quickly. For captions, using `<track default>` should work well on an English-only landing page.\n\nNow I'm considering the hero section's CTAs—adding a \"Watch the tour · 1:14\" link alongside \"Meet a persona\" and \"Start your own fitting\" feels like too many actions, so I'm leaning toward making it a secondary link with a small play icon rather than a full third button.\n\nClicking it scrolls to the tour section via Lenis (accounting for the fixed header offset), and since the click counts as a user gesture, I can safely autoplay the video with sound enabled rather than muted.\n\nGiven browser autoplay rules, calling play() synchronously inside the click handler is safest to preserve the user gesture, since Safari requires playback to start within the event handler itself. I'll wire up a document-level click listener in Qwik that intercepts clicks on the tour link and triggers playback immediately.\n\nI'll just catch play() failures silently and fall back to letting the user click manually, guarding so it only triggers if the video is actually paused — this feels like a reasonable, not over-engineered, \"Watch the tour\" behavior. I also want to extend the docs page's recording signal to include a 'tour' option as the default narrated recording alongside the existing locale choices.\n\nFor the French docs page, the tour keeps English narration but switches subtitles to French, consistent with how the current recording already behaves. I'm also mapping out which chapters get video clips versus static screenshots — tokens, generate, and new-persona have specific screen assets, while conversation has no screen at all.\n\nFor the clip videos themselves, I'm thinking about whether they should autoplay muted and loop, especially since chapters are collapsed inside closed `<details>` elements by default. I'll probably follow a pattern like the still-image-with-play-button approach, only autoplaying when the section is open, in view, and reduced-motion preferences are respected.\n\nBut autoplaying content over 5 seconds requires a pause control under WCAG 2.2.2, so I'd need a visible pause/play toggle if I go that route. The simpler and safer option is to just show a still frame with a \"Watch it\" button that the user clicks explicitly — avoids accessibility pitfalls and saves bandwidth, even if it's slightly less immediately engaging.\n\nThinking about which fits \"designer-grade polish\" better, I lean toward autoplay-when-visible with a pause toggle and reduced-motion respect, since that's the pattern Linear and Vercel docs use, and the pause control satisfies WCAG 2.2.2. I'll build a GuideClip component for this, a silent recording that plays while on screen unless motion is reduced, with a pause control.\n\nWriting intersection observer logic...\n\nBut I realize useVisibleTask$'s default intersection-observer strategy won't trigger inside a closed `<details>` element since it isn't visible there, so I need to account for that edge case.\n\nI'll also sync playing state with the video's play/pause events directly, so the button stays accurate regardless of trigger source. For the toggle button itself, I'll avoid the anti-pattern of pairing aria-pressed with a changing label — just use a changing label like \"Pause recording\"/\"Play recording\" positioned over the bottom-left of the video.\n\nFor the caption and chapter content, I'm checking the file date (archive.webm, Oct 10, 2026) against the displayed text to confirm consistency, but noticing the guide's \"jobs are simulated and no assets are generated\" phrasing conflicts with the actual demo behavior of replaying a real recorded run — I should adjust the clip caption to accurately describe what's shown, something like \"Studio demo replaying a recorded fal run.\"\n\nThen I'm matching each remaining chapter against its clip: tokens tab cycling through CSS/Tailwind/Panda formats, the home page conversation with Ren about preferences and colors, and the New Persona form where \"The Night Potter\" name and description are typed in — all align correctly with their chapter descriptions. For the fitting/orientation chapter I'm checking that the current screens reference the fitting.png landing page as the tour's starting point.\n\nI'm deciding where to swap clips into each chapter: the static tokens.png still gets replaced by the clip since both are from the same date, the outdated Oct 2 generate/new-persona screenshots get replaced since controls have moved since then, and the conversation chapter gets its first image added via the clip. I'm also restructuring the recording signal to support 'tour', 'current', 'en', and 'fr' modes, defaulting to the tour video source.\n\nI'm sketching captions and poster handling for the tour mode, plus bilingual notes describing the narration and subtitle language, and a transcript structure using timestamped headings with English and French paragraph text — deciding to keep timestamps as plain text rather than seekable buttons for simplicity. I also need to revise the docs' \"Watch a fitting\" aside to introduce the shorter tour before pointing to the longer narrated versions.\n\nNow I'm working out naming to avoid confusion between the new short tour and the existing \"Narrated tour\" buttons, considering labels like \"The tour\" for the new one and \"Walkthrough · English/Français\" for the old ones, while preserving the French button's language attribute and the historical note about the October 2026 recording that references OpenPencil and Material's interface changes.\n\nFinishing the French aside text, then checking the header copy. I'm also noting that the video element is set to a 16:10 aspect ratio while the actual tour footage is 16:9, so it'll end up letterboxed within that container.\n\nSetting aspect ratio dynamically...\n\nI also need download buttons for the recording and captions, and should update the developer docs to cover the fal provider plus a new \"how the tour video is made\" section, along with README and development docs updates, then wire up the landing page tour section and hero link.\n\nNo need to add og:video meta tags. For hash-based navigation like /docs/#tour, the tour recording is already the default so that's fine. Let me read through the full developer docs file to match its style before adding the new sections.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":433,"cache_read_input_tokens":229320,"output_tokens":9341,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":9257},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":433,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":9341,"cache_read_input_tokens":229320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":433,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":433},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":95118,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"f937326f-1ab1-4712-af32-851853967d33","timestamp":"2026-10-13T21:07:58.866Z","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"f937326f-1ab1-4712-af32-851853967d33","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfzvAAktwJodAnjXRXqD7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018paggzENjWgACrY3tw7S5m","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/site/src/routes/docs/developers/index.tsx"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":433,"cache_read_input_tokens":229320,"output_tokens":9341,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":9257},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":433,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":9341,"cache_read_input_tokens":229320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":433,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":433},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018paggzENjWgACrY3tw7S5m":{"file_path":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/site/src/routes/docs/developers/index.tsx"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"4954c284-4d94-4e41-bbf8-76b5dd133b42","timestamp":"2026-10-13T21:07:58.870Z","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"parentUuid":"4954c284-4d94-4e41-bbf8-76b5dd133b42","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4f5ed931-9f1d-475a-be32-8d6a81df3ec7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018paggzENjWgACrY3tw7S5m","type":"tool_result","content":"1\timport { component$ } from '@builder.io/qwik';\n2\timport type { DocumentHead } from '@builder.io/qwik-city';\n3\timport { css } from 'styled-system/css';\n4\timport { Nav, GITHUB_URL } from '~/components/layout/nav';\n5\timport { Footer } from '~/components/layout/footer';\n6\timport { CodeView } from '~/components/ui/code-view';\n7\timport { CopyCommand } from '~/components/ui/copy-command';\n8\timport { DocsNavigation } from '~/components/docs/navigation';\n9\timport { COMMANDS, API } from '~/components/docs/reference';\n10\t\n11\tconst SECTIONS = [\n12\t  ['development', 'Run locally'], ['notorganic', 'Account server'],\n13\t  ['providers', 'Local providers'], ['voice', 'Companion voice'], ['cli', 'CLI reference'], ['api', 'API reference'], ['outputs', 'Outputs'],\n14\t] as const;\n15\t\n16\tconst prose = css({ maxW: '70ch', fontSize: '15px', lineHeight: 1.7, color: 'ink-soft' });\n17\tconst section = css({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: 5, py: 9, borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.rule)', scrollMarginTop: '90px', minW: 0 });\n18\tconst title = css({ fontFamily: 'display', fontSize: '32px', fontWeight: 500, lineHeight: 1.15 });\n19\tconst table = css({ w: '100%', borderCollapse: 'collapse', fontSize: '13px', '& td, & th': { textAlign: 'left', py: 3, px: 3, borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.rule)', verticalAlign: 'top' }, '& th': { color: 'ink', fontWeight: 600 }, '& td:first-child': { fontFamily: 'mono', color: 'ink', minW: '210px' } });\n20\t\n21\texport default component$(() => (\n22\t  <>\n23\t    <Nav />\n24\t    <main id=\"main\" class={css({ maxW: '1180px', mx: 'auto', px: { base: 4, md: 8 }, pt: { base: 8, md: 14 } })}>\n25\t      <DocsNavigation active=\"developers\" />\n26\t      <header class={css({ maxW: '780px', pb: 10 })}>\n27\t        <h1 class=\"display\" style={{ fontSize: 'clamp(40px, 6vw, 72px)', lineHeight: 1.05 }}>Developer docs.</h1>\n28\t        <p class={css({ mt: 5, fontSize: '19px', lineHeight: 1.55, maxW: '58ch', color: 'ink-soft' })}>Run your own Baste installation, configure generation services, or build with the CLI and API.</p>\n29\t      <p class={prose}>Looking for help with personas, materials, moodboards or exports? <a href=\"/docs/\">Read the Studio guide</a>.</p>\n30\t      </header>\n31\t      <div class={css({ display: 'grid', gridTemplateColumns: { base: 'minmax(0, 1fr)', lg: '200px minmax(0, 1fr)' }, gap: { base: 3, lg: 12 }, alignItems: 'start' })}>\n32\t        <nav aria-label=\"Developer documentation sections\" class={css({ position: { lg: 'sticky' }, top: '90px', display: 'flex', flexDirection: { base: 'row', lg: 'column' }, flexWrap: 'wrap', gap: 3, fontSize: '14px', py: 4, borderTop: '1px solid token(colors.rule)' })}>\n33\t          {SECTIONS.map(([id, label]) => <a key={id} href={`#${id}`} class={css({ color: 'ink-soft', textDecoration: 'none', _hover: { color: 'thread-ink', textDecoration: 'underline' } })}>{label}</a>)}\n34\t        </nav>\n35\t        <div class={css({ minW: 0 })}>\n36\t          <section id=\"development\" class={section}>\n37\t            <h2 class={title}>Run locally</h2>\n38\t            <p class={prose}>From a checkout with Nix and Devenv installed, enter the development shell, install the locked dependencies, then start the API and site together.</p>\n39\t            <CodeView label=\"Start local development\" code={'devenv shell\\ndevenv tasks run baste:install\\ndevenv up'} />\n40\t            <p class={prose}>The Studio runs at <a href=\"http://localhost:5173/gui/\">localhost:5173/gui/</a>. Its same-origin <code>/api</code> proxy reaches the Baste server on port 3456. Set <code>BASTE_SITE_PORT</code> and <code>BASTE_API_PORT</code> to change the ports. Storybook is an optional process on port 6006.</p>\n41\t            <p class={prose}>Use the native tasks for individual commands and checks. The full task list and environment setup live in the <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/docs/development.md`}>development guide</a>.</p>\n42\t            <CopyCommand command=\"devenv tasks list\" />\n43\t          </section>\n44\t\n45\t          <section id=\"notorganic\" class={section}>\n46\t            <h2 class={title}>Configure an account server</h2>\n47\t            <p class={prose}>In Studio Settings, select <strong>Sign in / create profile</strong>. Not Organic creates or authenticates your account profile; a Baste design persona is a separate object. Manage your handle and account in the linked Not Organic portal.</p>\n48\t            <p class={prose}>The Baste server holds the provider session and DPoP key. The browser receives an HttpOnly Baste session cookie, a CSRF token and the profile's public DID and handle. The model picker loads the authorized catalog from your connected account.</p>\n49\t            <p class={prose}>Your wallet shows verified available credit and only the offers advertised by the provider. Select an offer to open checkout and review its current price before paying. On return, refresh the wallet: a redirect alone does not prove payment or credit delivery.</p>\n50\t            <p class={prose}>Hosted generation currently covers image suites and asset judging. Full UI kits still need your local SVG and video providers. An unverified, blocked or empty wallet prevents paid hosted generation; prompt drafts remain available.</p>\n51\t            <p class={prose}>Set a maximum spend for each hosted run, initially $1. The live budget plan counts image and judging requests, including QD candidates and final outputs. Reduce iterations, batch size or outputs per purpose until the plan fits your maximum and the server limit, or explicitly raise your maximum. These conservative ceilings are spending limits, not quoted prices.</p>\n52\t            <p class={prose}>For server operators: enable <code>NOTORGANIC_ENABLED</code>, set <code>BASTE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN</code>, and configure the issuer and authorization URL. Baste must be registered with Not Organic for its origin, callback, product audience, scopes and billing offers. Public checkout needs an HTTPS return origin.</p>\n53\t            <CodeView label=\"Account server configuration\" code={'NOTORGANIC_ENABLED=true\\nBASTE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173\\nNOTORGANIC_ISSUER=https://api.notorganic.info\\nNOTORGANIC_AUTHORIZATION_URL=https://id.notorganic.info/authorize'} />\n54\t            <p class={prose}>The callback is <code>/api/notorganic/callback</code> on the Studio origin. Production hosting must proxy <code>/api</code> to the Baste server. Sessions live in server memory and expire after eight hours; restarting the server requires signing in again. Read the <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/docs/notorganic.md`}>account integration guide</a> for the deployment boundary.</p>\n55\t          </section>\n56\t\n57\t          <section id=\"providers\" class={section}>\n58\t            <h2 class={title}>Use your own provider keys</h2>\n59\t            <p class={prose}>Local mode keeps provider keys in the server environment or local config. Image generation uses OpenAI or Google; SVG generation uses QuiverAI; video uses its configured provider. The Studio never needs an upstream key pasted into a form.</p>\n60\t            <CodeView label=\"Local provider key configuration\" code={'OPENAI_API_KEY=…\\nQUIVER_API_KEY=…\\nGOOGLE_API_KEY=…'} />\n61\t            <p class={prose}>Copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code> and configure only the services you use. Run <code>baste config --init</code> to create <code>baste.config.json</code>. Model IDs must be supported by your chosen provider. Local generation and CLI use this configuration; hosted Studio requests use the connected account session.</p>\n62\t          </section>\n63\t\n64\t          <section id=\"voice\" class={section}>\n65\t            <h2 class={title}>Companion voice</h2>\n66\t            <p class={prose}>Set <code>FAL_KEY</code> on the Baste API server for ElevenLabs Turbo v2.5 speech and Wizper transcription. The browser sends recorded audio to Baste and receives audio bytes or editable text; it never receives the provider key. Recording starts only after a user gesture and stops after 45 seconds.</p>\n67\t            <CodeView label=\"Start local voice with Skate\" code={'node scripts/runtime.mjs api --voice-from-skate'} />\n68\t            <p class={prose}>The Skate option reads <code>fal_api_key@secrets</code> into the API process environment. Alternatively set <code>FAL_KEY</code> in your server environment. The fal account needs available credit. Set <code>BASTE_VOICE_ENABLED=false</code> to disable voice.</p>\n69\t            <p class={prose}>Hosted account mode additionally requires <code>BASTE_VOICE_ENABLED=true</code>. Existing account authentication, origin and CSRF checks apply. Voice uses the operator’s fal balance, separately from Not Organic wallet billing. Each server process allows ten requests per caller per minute, 120 total per hour, and four concurrent requests; replicated deployments need a shared limit before increasing capacity.</p>\n70\t            <p class={prose}>Speech and transcription use bounded, cancellable requests. Audio is not saved by Baste. fal receives the audio and text, with provider request storage disabled. Spoken replies require an explicit opt-in. Pause, resume, stop, persona changes and tab visibility control playback and microphone cleanup.</p>\n71\t            <p class={prose}>This is turn-based voice with editable transcripts. The planned GPT-Live design partner remains a separate integration. Voice does not turn the authored guided demo into a model conversation.</p>\n72\t          </section>\n73\t\n74\t          <section id=\"cli\" class={section}>\n75\t            <h2 class={title}>CLI reference</h2>\n76\t            <p class={prose}>The CLI operates on your local persona store. It does not inherit a Studio account session. Use <code>baste --help</code> for versioning, remix, site decomposition, cultural references and OpenPencil commands.</p>\n77\t            <div class={css({ overflowX: 'auto' })} tabIndex={0} role=\"region\" aria-label=\"CLI commands\"><table class={table}><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody>{COMMANDS.map(([command, description]) => <tr key={command}><td>{command}</td><td>{description}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>\n78\t          </section>\n79\t\n80\t          <section id=\"api\" class={section}>\n81\t            <h2 class={title}>API reference</h2>\n82\t            <p class={prose}>The development site proxies these same-origin routes to the local API. Account routes require a connected session. In account mode, personas and generation results are scoped to the authenticated DID. Mutations require <code>X-Baste-CSRF</code> from the status response.</p>\n83\t            <div class={css({ overflowX: 'auto' })} tabIndex={0} role=\"region\" aria-label=\"API endpoints\"><table class={table}><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody>{API.map(([route, description]) => <tr key={route}><td>{route}</td><td>{description}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>\n84\t            <p class={prose}>Errors distinguish authentication (401), denied access (403), insufficient credit (402), busy provider (429), and unavailable services. Config writes and native file/watch operations are unavailable in account mode. The <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}#readme`}>README</a> contains the wider local API and TypeScript package usage.</p>\n85\t          </section>\n86\t\n87\t          <section id=\"outputs\" class={section}>\n88\t            <h2 class={title}>Keep the fitting</h2>\n89\t            <p class={prose}>Local output lives under <code>assets/output/</code>: SVGs, images, videos, plus a persona suite manifest with metadata and relationships. In account mode, persona records and assets belong to the authenticated DID's separate directory.</p>\n90\t            <CodeView label=\"Local asset output structure\" code={'assets/output/\\n  svg/\\n  images/\\n  videos/\\n  {persona}-suite.json'} />\n91\t            <p class={prose}>The quality-diversity archive explores color temperature, visual density and abstractness. Judging weights persona alignment at 30%, visual quality and uniqueness at 20% each, coherence and usability at 15% each. These scores guide selection; review assets in the interface they will serve.</p>\n92\t          </section>\n93\t        </div>\n94\t      </div>\n95\t    </main>\n96\t    <Footer />\n97\t  </>\n98\t));\n99\t\n100\texport const head: DocumentHead = {\n101\t  title: 'Developer docs · Baste',\n102\t  meta: [{ name: 'description', content: 'Install and operate Baste with Devenv, configure local and hosted providers, and use the CLI and API.' }],\n103\t};\n104\t"}]},"uuid":"4df39869-f8ba-429c-ad73-abe05e522e34","timestamp":"2026-10-13T21:07:58.880Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/site/src/routes/docs/developers/index.tsx","content":"import { component$ } from '@builder.io/qwik';\nimport type { DocumentHead } from '@builder.io/qwik-city';\nimport { css } from 'styled-system/css';\nimport { Nav, GITHUB_URL } from '~/components/layout/nav';\nimport { Footer } from '~/components/layout/footer';\nimport { CodeView } from '~/components/ui/code-view';\nimport { CopyCommand } from '~/components/ui/copy-command';\nimport { DocsNavigation } from '~/components/docs/navigation';\nimport { COMMANDS, API } from '~/components/docs/reference';\n\nconst SECTIONS = [\n  ['development', 'Run locally'], ['notorganic', 'Account server'],\n  ['providers', 'Local providers'], ['voice', 'Companion voice'], ['cli', 'CLI reference'], ['api', 'API reference'], ['outputs', 'Outputs'],\n] as const;\n\nconst prose = css({ maxW: '70ch', fontSize: '15px', lineHeight: 1.7, color: 'ink-soft' });\nconst section = css({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: 5, py: 9, borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.rule)', scrollMarginTop: '90px', minW: 0 });\nconst title = css({ fontFamily: 'display', fontSize: '32px', fontWeight: 500, lineHeight: 1.15 });\nconst table = css({ w: '100%', borderCollapse: 'collapse', fontSize: '13px', '& td, & th': { textAlign: 'left', py: 3, px: 3, borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.rule)', verticalAlign: 'top' }, '& th': { color: 'ink', fontWeight: 600 }, '& td:first-child': { fontFamily: 'mono', color: 'ink', minW: '210px' } });\n\nexport default component$(() => (\n  <>\n    <Nav />\n    <main id=\"main\" class={css({ maxW: '1180px', mx: 'auto', px: { base: 4, md: 8 }, pt: { base: 8, md: 14 } })}>\n      <DocsNavigation active=\"developers\" />\n      <header class={css({ maxW: '780px', pb: 10 })}>\n        <h1 class=\"display\" style={{ fontSize: 'clamp(40px, 6vw, 72px)', lineHeight: 1.05 }}>Developer docs.</h1>\n        <p class={css({ mt: 5, fontSize: '19px', lineHeight: 1.55, maxW: '58ch', color: 'ink-soft' })}>Run your own Baste installation, configure generation services, or build with the CLI and API.</p>\n      <p class={prose}>Looking for help with personas, materials, moodboards or exports? <a href=\"/docs/\">Read the Studio guide</a>.</p>\n      </header>\n      <div class={css({ display: 'grid', gridTemplateColumns: { base: 'minmax(0, 1fr)', lg: '200px minmax(0, 1fr)' }, gap: { base: 3, lg: 12 }, alignItems: 'start' })}>\n        <nav aria-label=\"Developer documentation sections\" class={css({ position: { lg: 'sticky' }, top: '90px', display: 'flex', flexDirection: { base: 'row', lg: 'column' }, flexWrap: 'wrap', gap: 3, fontSize: '14px', py: 4, borderTop: '1px solid token(colors.rule)' })}>\n          {SECTIONS.map(([id, label]) => <a key={id} href={`#${id}`} class={css({ color: 'ink-soft', textDecoration: 'none', _hover: { color: 'thread-ink', textDecoration: 'underline' } })}>{label}</a>)}\n        </nav>\n        <div class={css({ minW: 0 })}>\n          <section id=\"development\" class={section}>\n            <h2 class={title}>Run locally</h2>\n            <p class={prose}>From a checkout with Nix and Devenv installed, enter the development shell, install the locked dependencies, then start the API and site together.</p>\n            <CodeView label=\"Start local development\" code={'devenv shell\\ndevenv tasks run baste:install\\ndevenv up'} />\n            <p class={prose}>The Studio runs at <a href=\"http://localhost:5173/gui/\">localhost:5173/gui/</a>. Its same-origin <code>/api</code> proxy reaches the Baste server on port 3456. Set <code>BASTE_SITE_PORT</code> and <code>BASTE_API_PORT</code> to change the ports. Storybook is an optional process on port 6006.</p>\n            <p class={prose}>Use the native tasks for individual commands and checks. The full task list and environment setup live in the <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/docs/development.md`}>development guide</a>.</p>\n            <CopyCommand command=\"devenv tasks list\" />\n          </section>\n\n          <section id=\"notorganic\" class={section}>\n            <h2 class={title}>Configure an account server</h2>\n            <p class={prose}>In Studio Settings, select <strong>Sign in / create profile</strong>. Not Organic creates or authenticates your account profile; a Baste design persona is a separate object. Manage your handle and account in the linked Not Organic portal.</p>\n            <p class={prose}>The Baste server holds the provider session and DPoP key. The browser receives an HttpOnly Baste session cookie, a CSRF token and the profile's public DID and handle. The model picker loads the authorized catalog from your connected account.</p>\n            <p class={prose}>Your wallet shows verified available credit and only the offers advertised by the provider. Select an offer to open checkout and review its current price before paying. On return, refresh the wallet: a redirect alone does not prove payment or credit delivery.</p>\n            <p class={prose}>Hosted generation currently covers image suites and asset judging. Full UI kits still need your local SVG and video providers. An unverified, blocked or empty wallet prevents paid hosted generation; prompt drafts remain available.</p>\n            <p class={prose}>Set a maximum spend for each hosted run, initially $1. The live budget plan counts image and judging requests, including QD candidates and final outputs. Reduce iterations, batch size or outputs per purpose until the plan fits your maximum and the server limit, or explicitly raise your maximum. These conservative ceilings are spending limits, not quoted prices.</p>\n            <p class={prose}>For server operators: enable <code>NOTORGANIC_ENABLED</code>, set <code>BASTE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN</code>, and configure the issuer and authorization URL. Baste must be registered with Not Organic for its origin, callback, product audience, scopes and billing offers. Public checkout needs an HTTPS return origin.</p>\n            <CodeView label=\"Account server configuration\" code={'NOTORGANIC_ENABLED=true\\nBASTE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173\\nNOTORGANIC_ISSUER=https://api.notorganic.info\\nNOTORGANIC_AUTHORIZATION_URL=https://id.notorganic.info/authorize'} />\n            <p class={prose}>The callback is <code>/api/notorganic/callback</code> on the Studio origin. Production hosting must proxy <code>/api</code> to the Baste server. Sessions live in server memory and expire after eight hours; restarting the server requires signing in again. Read the <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/docs/notorganic.md`}>account integration guide</a> for the deployment boundary.</p>\n          </section>\n\n          <section id=\"providers\" class={section}>\n            <h2 class={title}>Use your own provider keys</h2>\n            <p class={prose}>Local mode keeps provider keys in the server environment or local config. Image generation uses OpenAI or Google; SVG generation uses QuiverAI; video uses its configured provider. The Studio never needs an upstream key pasted into a form.</p>\n            <CodeView label=\"Local provider key configuration\" code={'OPENAI_API_KEY=…\\nQUIVER_API_KEY=…\\nGOOGLE_API_KEY=…'} />\n            <p class={prose}>Copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code> and configure only the services you use. Run <code>baste config --init</code> to create <code>baste.config.json</code>. Model IDs must be supported by your chosen provider. Local generation and CLI use this configuration; hosted Studio requests use the connected account session.</p>\n          </section>\n\n          <section id=\"voice\" class={section}>\n            <h2 class={title}>Companion voice</h2>\n            <p class={prose}>Set <code>FAL_KEY</code> on the Baste API server for ElevenLabs Turbo v2.5 speech and Wizper transcription. The browser sends recorded audio to Baste and receives audio bytes or editable text; it never receives the provider key. Recording starts only after a user gesture and stops after 45 seconds.</p>\n            <CodeView label=\"Start local voice with Skate\" code={'node scripts/runtime.mjs api --voice-from-skate'} />\n            <p class={prose}>The Skate option reads <code>fal_api_key@secrets</code> into the API process environment. Alternatively set <code>FAL_KEY</code> in your server environment. The fal account needs available credit. Set <code>BASTE_VOICE_ENABLED=false</code> to disable voice.</p>\n            <p class={prose}>Hosted account mode additionally requires <code>BASTE_VOICE_ENABLED=true</code>. Existing account authentication, origin and CSRF checks apply. Voice uses the operator’s fal balance, separately from Not Organic wallet billing. Each server process allows ten requests per caller per minute, 120 total per hour, and four concurrent requests; replicated deployments need a shared limit before increasing capacity.</p>\n            <p class={prose}>Speech and transcription use bounded, cancellable requests. Audio is not saved by Baste. fal receives the audio and text, with provider request storage disabled. Spoken replies require an explicit opt-in. Pause, resume, stop, persona changes and tab visibility control playback and microphone cleanup.</p>\n            <p class={prose}>This is turn-based voice with editable transcripts. The planned GPT-Live design partner remains a separate integration. Voice does not turn the authored guided demo into a model conversation.</p>\n          </section>\n\n          <section id=\"cli\" class={section}>\n            <h2 class={title}>CLI reference</h2>\n            <p class={prose}>The CLI operates on your local persona store. It does not inherit a Studio account session. Use <code>baste --help</code> for versioning, remix, site decomposition, cultural references and OpenPencil commands.</p>\n            <div class={css({ overflowX: 'auto' })} tabIndex={0} role=\"region\" aria-label=\"CLI commands\"><table class={table}><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody>{COMMANDS.map(([command, description]) => <tr key={command}><td>{command}</td><td>{description}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>\n          </section>\n\n          <section id=\"api\" class={section}>\n            <h2 class={title}>API reference</h2>\n            <p class={prose}>The development site proxies these same-origin routes to the local API. Account routes require a connected session. In account mode, personas and generation results are scoped to the authenticated DID. Mutations require <code>X-Baste-CSRF</code> from the status response.</p>\n            <div class={css({ overflowX: 'auto' })} tabIndex={0} role=\"region\" aria-label=\"API endpoints\"><table class={table}><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody>{API.map(([route, description]) => <tr key={route}><td>{route}</td><td>{description}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>\n            <p class={prose}>Errors distinguish authentication (401), denied access (403), insufficient credit (402), busy provider (429), and unavailable services. Config writes and native file/watch operations are unavailable in account mode. The <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}#readme`}>README</a> contains the wider local API and TypeScript package usage.</p>\n          </section>\n\n          <section id=\"outputs\" class={section}>\n            <h2 class={title}>Keep the fitting</h2>\n            <p class={prose}>Local output lives under <code>assets/output/</code>: SVGs, images, videos, plus a persona suite manifest with metadata and relationships. In account mode, persona records and assets belong to the authenticated DID's separate directory.</p>\n            <CodeView label=\"Local asset output structure\" code={'assets/output/\\n  svg/\\n  images/\\n  videos/\\n  {persona}-suite.json'} />\n            <p class={prose}>The quality-diversity archive explores color temperature, visual density and abstractness. Judging weights persona alignment at 30%, visual quality and uniqueness at 20% each, coherence and usability at 15% each. These scores guide selection; review assets in the interface they will serve.</p>\n          </section>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </main>\n    <Footer />\n  </>\n));\n\nexport const head: DocumentHead = {\n  title: 'Developer docs · Baste',\n  meta: [{ name: 'description', content: 'Install and operate Baste with Devenv, configure local and hosted providers, and use the CLI and API.' }],\n};\n","numLines":104,"startLine":1,"totalLines":104}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4954c284-4d94-4e41-bbf8-76b5dd133b42","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hopcroft"}
{"type":"last-prompt","lastPrompt":"Note: the T3 server restarted, and this background work was cancelled before it finished. It will not report back: - shell: Render the narrated tour to MP4 (8 workers) (id b5d60z69n)  Continue where y…","leafUuid":"4df39869-f8ba-429c-ad73-abe05e522e34","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1"}
{"type":"ai-title","aiTitle":"Lenis scroll and conversational persona chat","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1"}
{"type":"mode","mode":"normal","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1"}
{"type":"atis-latch","atis":"7b899e07c71b9807","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1"}
{"parentUuid":"4df39869-f8ba-429c-ad73-abe05e522e34","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfzvHP41k14n213NXYiys","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm now designing the TourSection for the landing page, aiming for an atelier aesthetic with a mono label, Bodoni display heading, and red thread motif tied to the overall paper-craft feel.\n\nI'm drafting the copy itself — settling on a heading like \"Watch a fitting, start to finish.\" paired with a short paragraph describing the measure → fit → cut flow, recorded in the real Studio with English narration.\n\nFor the video treatment, I'm planning a simple framed 16:9 player with subtle border and shadow, using a poster image with a custom play overlay instead of native controls so it feels intentional rather than generic — clicking reveals controls and plays with sound. I'm also sketching a row of clickable chapter buttons beneath it that seek to specific timestamps in the recording.\n\nLooking at the actual tour.json chapters (why, measure, fitting, baste, fit, cut, talk, yours), I'm weighing whether to show all 8 or mirror the video's own on-screen rail of 7 (Measure through Yours), since \"why\" functions more as a cold open than a real chapter.\n\nthe intro as the unnumbered start, filtering it out of the numbered index. I'll add a \"Read the narration\" details element with each chapter's transcript text for accessibility and SEO. For captions, I'm adding English and French track elements, thinking through whether the `default` attribute should make captions show automatically given the narration is already audible.\n\nLooking at the caption positioning values, I'm checking how the cue box centers near the bottom of the stage area without clashing with the paper background below it. I'm deciding a download link probably isn't needed on the landing page.\n\nI notice guide.css explicitly sets `cursor: pointer` on summary and button elements, which actually contradicts the global rule that components should never set cursor themselves — I should flag that inconsistency.\n\nClicking a chapter should seek and start playback if not already playing, since it's a user gesture so sound is fine, and also reveal the controls. I don't need to pause on scroll-out — keeping the video playing when scrolled away matches typical site behavior. Lenis scrolling over the native video controls should work fine too. Now I'm starting the Qwik component implementation, setting up a time-formatting helper and pulling in the tour data...\n\nStill writing component...\n\nGoing with no-controls-plus-custom-button since that's the established idiom here; since the video is narrated, native controls after start give users scrubbing and volume. I'm giving the overlay button a descriptive accessible name with duration, keeping the video itself non-focusable until started, setting a poster with `preload=\"none\"` for bandwidth savings, and adding explicit width/height for aspect ratio.\n\nFor layout I'm considering a full-width video under the header rather than a two-column split, echoing the hero's structure, with chapters shown as a horizontal tape-measure timeline beneath it — segments proportional to duration, tick marks, yellow tape accent, and a red thread fill showing playback progress.\n\nI'm sketching the chapter segments as a scrubber bar with each labeled chapter stretching proportionally to its length (measure, fitting room, baste, fit, cut, talk, yours), with clicking a segment seeking to that point in the video.\n\nEach segment becomes a button styled like a tape measure, with a thin ruled border, tick marks, and a red thread-colored progress fill that grows across the active segment as playback advances, driven by a CSS variable tracking elapsed time within that segment's range.\n\nI can set the CSS variable imperatively in the timeupdate handler, but since that only fires ~4Hz, I'll add a CSS transition on transform to smooth out the stepping. For mobile under 768px, I'm thinking segments should become horizontally scrollable with a minimum width rather than trying to cram seven across, which raises the question of how that interacts with nested Lenis scroll behavior.\n\nI'll opt for a vertical row layout on mobile instead, with each chapter showing number, name, and time compactly, letting the fill still work as a bottom bar indicator. For marking the active chapter, I'm deciding between aria-current values like \"step\" or \"true\" — I'll go with \"true\" for simplicity, and I need a signal that updates reactively whenever the current chapter changes based on playback time.\n\nBelow the chapters I'll add a collapsible transcript section with the narration paragraphs in English, plus a note about captions being available in English and French, and a link out to the Studio guide docs. For the hero section, I'm considering whether to add a third CTA like \"Watch the tour (1:14)\" alongside the existing \"Meet a persona\" button and \"Start your own fitting\" link, though three calls-to-action might be too cluttered — I'll keep \"Meet a persona\" as primary and figure out where tour link fits.\n\nI'm deciding to keep both existing CTAs and add the tour link as a secondary text link, styled with a small play icon matching the one used elsewhere, rather than placing the tour section awkwardly between the hero and the persona section since the anchor link already skips past it fine.\n\nFor placement, I'm thinking the tour should sit right after the hero and before the meet section, considering whether to differentiate it with a darker \"paper-deep\" band background to frame the video like a cutting table, since the hero and tour both sit on the same paper tone.\n\nI should check contrast ratios for text colors against this paper-deep background — ink-soft holds up well but ink-muted is borderline for small text under AA standards, so I'll default to ink-soft for anything small. I also want to double check that the earlier flicker/resize background bug was actually resolved.\n\nSections with their own solid background, like meet-section's #f8f6f0, are already an accepted pattern that would just sit on top of any page-level shader effect, so that's fine. For the studio guide docs page, I'm adding a 'tour' recording option and making it the default, wiring up its video, captions by language, and poster source.\n\nNow I need to figure out labeling since the existing buttons already use \"Narrated tour\" naming for the older October recordings — I want to pick a distinct label for the new 1:14 tour button that doesn't collide, something like \"The tour\" or \"Baste in a minute,\" and work out matching translations for the French version.\n\nFor captions, I'm setting up track elements with the right language attribute and label, and considering whether to provide both English and French subtitle tracks simultaneously with the current UI language set as default. I also need to account for the new tour video's 16:9 aspect ratio differing from the other clips' dimensions.\n\nI should update the aside text too, mentioning the 74-second summary alongside the worktable and the longer tour, with matching French translation. The tour transcript should use chapters with h3 titles and paragraphs, with titleFr/textFr for French, and downloads should offer the video plus captions in the current language — I'm also wondering if chapter seek buttons belong in the guide.\n\nI'll skip seeking in the guide since the landing tape already covers that, though showing timestamps next to chapter titles in the transcript could be a nice touch. For chapter clips, I'm mapping specific chapters like generate, tokens, conversation, and new-persona to a figure element rendering a clip component when present.\n\nOn interaction, I'm weighing autoplay-when-visible muted loops against an explicit play button like the MotionStudy pattern. Since chapters are collapsed `<details>` elements by default, content stays hidden until opened, which makes autoplay-on-reveal reasonable, but I lean toward the explicit play button for predictability and accessibility reasons.\n\nI'll go with a still image default plus a play button overlay that toggles to pause, matching the MotionStudy pattern, and extract this into a shared `ClipPlayer` component within the docs folder so both the guide and graphite-studies can reuse it without duplicating styles.\n\nI need to decide which chapters get outdated screenshots swapped for clips — generate, conversation, and new-persona all need updates. For captions, I'm thinking per-clip descriptions would serve accessibility better than a generic recording date label, so I'm drafting bilingual captions that describe each clip's actual content rather than just when it was recorded.\n\nI'm double-checking the conversation clip timing — it shows the Moss chat, then Ren's tab click, greeting, and the \"Why these colours?\" reply with palette swatches at the end, confirming the clip still lines up. I also want to verify the cursor system respects the pointer rule for buttons defined globally.\n\nFound the bug: guide.css's `.guide-watch button {cursor:pointer}` overrides the global pin-cursor rule, which is exactly the inconsistency the user flagged. I'll remove that override since `summary` and `button` already inherit the pin cursor from the global `:where(...)` selector — I just need to confirm `summary` is included in that pin-cursor list before deleting the line.\n\nNow I'm turning to the developer docs, where I need to add a `fal` provider section explaining it can run every pipeline stage with one API key (Seedream v4 for images, Recraft v3 for SVG, Seedance 1 Pro for video, plus a vision judge via any-llm), configured through FAL_KEY and either `--provider fal` or per-stage provider flags. I want to verify the exact model IDs by checking generators.ts rather than relying on what I saw in the demo video.\n\nI also want to document the \"Tour video\" rebuild workflow, walking through the pipeline steps (record, narrate, score, build, check, render, publish) with their corresponding script commands, plus platform notes about handling LD_LIBRARY_PATH on nix/guix hosts, the ldd shim for missing Chrome libraries, and the detached rendering env var needed when running from a background process.\n\nI should check the headers in narrate-tour.mjs and score-tour.mjs to confirm their exact flags and output paths before documenting them, and make sure \"Tour video\" gets added to the sections list alongside providers like fal. Then for README.md, I want to add a short mention of the tour near the top pointing to the video file and site link, and verify what's already documented there about the fal provider before expanding it.\n\nI should also plan to add a \"Tour video\" section to docs/development.md with the pipeline commands, and double-check the `.env.example` comment about FAL_KEY to mention the `--provider fal` flag too. For git tracking, the raw footage and renders in videos/baste-tour are large and shouldn't be committed, so I'm considering whether a .gitignore is needed there, though the final publish outputs in site/public/media/tour are smaller and already untracked.\n\nI'm leaning toward ignoring `renders/`, `.work/`, `snapshots/`, `footage-v1/`, and `music/bed-source.wav` since those are derived or obsolete, while leaving the raw `footage/` directory as a judgment call for the user since it's not trivially reproducible without re-recording against running servers.\n\nFor cleanup, deleting `footage-v1` (the superseded first take, ~157MB) and `bed-source.wav` (the raw audio download, 13MB) matches the pending task noted in the summary, though deletion is hard to reverse.\n\nFor verification, I'll run tsc, lint, build, and tests on the site, plus a root-level test run and a visual Playwright screenshot check.\n\nI need to launch the dev server (on port 5173 in site/) to capture screenshots of the homepage tour section, docs, and developers page at both desktop and mobile widths — using setsid nohup since T3 restarts kill background tasks, and stopping it afterward. I should also verify that site/src/lib/ exists and that resolveJsonModule is enabled in tsconfig, since the guide.json imports imply it should be.\n\nI'm cautious about `findLast` availability depending on tsconfig lib/target, so I'll just use a loop or reduce instead to be safe. I still need to read through several files: the global.css cursor block, the top of .env.example, README sections on providers, docs/development.md, the narrate/score script headers, fal model generators, tsconfig, site package.json scripts, and how guide.css loads.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":141974,"cache_read_input_tokens":92618,"output_tokens":11599,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":11385},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":141974,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":11599,"cache_read_input_tokens":92618,"cache_creation_input_tokens":141974,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":141974},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":116890,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"7b2e5b27-6da6-4ba9-aaa2-0d32dde6a646","timestamp":"2026-10-13T21:09:59.083Z","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/home/diogenes/Projects/apps/baste/videos/baste-tour","sessionId":"69afbb2a-630d-41bd-9b69-0c77564d8af1","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"toasty-puzzling-hop